-- title:
--   Conditional risk measure $\mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}$ of atom-wise sets $\mathfrak A_i$, display (2.7)
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F)$ be a measurable space and $\mathcal G$ the finite sigma subalgebra generated by a finite partition $\{\varpi_i\}_{i\in\mathcal I}$. For each $i\in\mathcal I$ let $\mathfrak A_i$ be a set of probability measures on $(\Omega,\mathcal F)$ (in the paper: nonempty, supported on $\varpi_i$). The **conditional risk measure** determined by the sets $\mathfrak A_i$ assigns to a variable $Z:\Omega\to\mathbb R$ the $\mathcal G$-measurable variable $\mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}(Z)$ with
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}(Z)(\varpi_i)=\sup_{Q\in\mathfrak A_i}\mathbb E_Q[Z],\qquad i\in\mathcal I .
--   $$
--
--   It is the risk-averse way of making a distributionally robust functional conditional: one ambiguity set per atom, rather than one set on $\Omega$ conditioned atom by atom as in (2.6).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The variable is represented by its atom values, a function $\mathcal I\to\overline{\mathbb R}$, and the supremum is taken in the extended reals ($-\infty$ for an empty $\mathfrak A_i$, $+\infty$ if unbounded). $\mathbb E_Q[Z]$ is the Bochner integral $\int Z\,dQ$. The requirements on $\mathfrak A_i$ (nonempty, probability measures, supported on $\varpi_i$) and the integrability of $Z$ are hypotheses of the theorems that use this functional.
-- source:
--   Shapiro & Pichler, Conditional Distributionally Robust Functionals, preprint (October 9, 2022), display (2.7), p.5

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory

namespace CondDRO

/-- **The conditional risk measure** R|𝒢 determined by sets 𝔄ᵢ of probability measures, one for
each atom ϖᵢ of a finite sigma algebra 𝒢 (Shapiro & Pichler, *Conditional Distributionally Robust
Functionals*, preprint (October 9, 2022), display (2.7), p.5):
`R|𝒢(Z)(ϖᵢ) := sup_{Q ∈ 𝔄ᵢ} E_Q[Z]`, `i ∈ ℐ`.

**Formalization Note.** The 𝒢-measurable variable R|𝒢(Z), constant on each atom, is represented
by its value on each atom, a function `I → EReal`. The supremum is taken in `EReal`: it is `⊥`
when `A i` is empty and `⊤` when the expectations are unbounded above. `E_Q[Z]` is Mathlib's
Bochner integral `∫ ω, Z ω ∂Q`; statements using this functional assume `Z` integrable for the
measures involved. The requirements on 𝔄ᵢ (nonempty, probability measures supported on ϖᵢ) are
hypotheses of the theorems, not part of this definition. -/
noncomputable def condRM {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {I : Type*}
    (A : I → Set (Measure Ω)) (Z : Ω → ℝ) : I → EReal :=
  fun i => ⨆ Q ∈ A i, ((∫ ω, Z ω ∂Q : ℝ) : EReal)

end CondDRO


